course: dictionary
lesson: 256
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. To find the right one, look at the clues around the word: the place, the people and what they are doing. Read the whole sentence before you choose.
explain: «The altar at the front of the church was covered with a white cloth.» The church and the cloth tell you altar means the holy table where the priest prepares the bread and wine for communion.
explain: «People in ancient times left gifts on an altar for their gods.» Now the clues are ancient times and gods, so altar means a table or stone used in an old religion. Same word, different place, different meaning.
explain: Altar is also part of an idiom. «I sacrifice this fair morning on the altar of duty and friendship!» says Toad. Nobody puts a morning on a table: it means he is giving up his morning for his friend. Idioms need the clues of the whole sentence.
fact: An altar is a holy table at the front of a church, where the priest or minister prepares communion.
fact: In old religions, people offered gifts or sacrifices to God or gods on an altar, which could be a table or a stone.
fact: Couples often stand before the altar at a wedding, and Bible stories tell of Abraham, Noah and Elijah building altars.
fact: «Lead someone to the altar» means to marry them.
fact: «Sacrifice something on the altar of something» means to give it up for that thing.
exercise: «The altar at the front of the church was covered with a white cloth.» Where is the altar? | *At the front of the church | In the school playground | Under the sea | At the top of a hill
exercise: Which phrase means a person is getting married? | *Lead someone to the altar | Sacrifice someone on the altar | Altar boy | Stand behind the pulpit
exercise: «Abraham, Noah and Elijah built altars.» What were altars used for in these old Bible stories? | As a seat for a king | To keep ships safe in harbour | To store cakes and biscuits | *To offer gifts or sacrifices to God
test: «I sacrifice this fair morning on the altar of duty and friendship!» What does this idiom suggest? | He is very angry with his friend | *He is giving up his morning to help his friend | He built a table out of stone | He wants to eat breakfast early
test: An ofrenda is an altar with photos, candles, food and favourite things of the dead. What is an ofrenda for? | To cook dinner for the family | To mark the start of a school term | To keep the rain away | *To show love and welcome the dead
exam: «The priest walked slowly to the altar to prepare the bread and wine.» Which meaning of altar fits best? | A book of Bible stories | *The holy table at the front of a church | A tall tower in a field | A kind of cake
